For the KTH combination, we also want a more specific regression check.
If the session is already authenticated to KTH, entering LADOK should
reuse that state rather than showing the UG password form or Microsoft's
MFA challenge again.

We add two small tracking subclasses for this live regression.

<<test functions>>=
class TrackingUGlogin(kth.UGlogin):
  def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):
    super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
    self.password_form_logins = 0

  def login(self, session, response, args=None, kwargs=None):
    if response.url.startswith(self.LOGIN_URL) and (
        'id="loginForm"' in response.text
        or "id='loginForm'" in response.text):
      self.password_form_logins += 1
    return super().login(session, response, args, kwargs)

class TrackingAzureMFA(microsoft.AzureMFA):
  def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):
    super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
    self.challenge_count = 0

  def login(self, session, response, args=None, kwargs=None):
    self.challenge_count += 1
    return super().login(session, response, args, kwargs)

@pytest.mark.live
@pytest.mark.ladok_live
def test_reuse_UG_session_for_LADOK_login():
  ug = TrackingUGlogin(
    os.environ["KTH_LOGIN"],
    os.environ["KTH_PASSWD"],
    "https://app.kth.se/ug-gruppeditor/"
  )
  mfa = TrackingAzureMFA(
    url="https://login.ug.kth.se",
    notification_cmd="notify-send"
  )
  session = weblogin.AutologinSession([
    ladok.SSOlogin("KTH Royal Institute of Technology"),
    kth.SAMLlogin(),
    ug,
    mfa
  ])

  kth_response = session.get("https://app.kth.se/ug-gruppeditor/")
  assert kth_response.status_code == requests.codes.ok and \
      kth_response.url.startswith("https://app.kth.se")

  ug.password_form_logins = 0
  mfa.challenge_count = 0

  response = make_ladok_request(session)
  assert_ladok_user_info_response(response)
  assert ug.password_form_logins == 0
  assert mfa.challenge_count == 0
@

\subsection{Log into any university}

Now, here is the trick.
If we don't have any other [[AutologinHandler]] objects handling the logins to 
the specific university, the [[ladok_response]] of
[[<<run return request with added institution SAML URL as entityID>>]] will 
contain a page in the login process of the university.
These pages just contain [[<form>...<input>...</input>...</form>]] elements 
that need to be posted.
In most cases, we just need the default values.
The only time we need to add values, are for the username and password fields.
Fortunately, those are most often called [[username]] and [[password]] by the 
universities, so we can look for them and fill them when they show up.

However, we can do better.
We can take a dictionary where the keys match variable names in these forms, if 
we have a match, we use the value as value when we post the form.
This way, for most universities we can simply do:
\begin{minted}{json}
{
  "username": "dbosk",
  "password": "my secret password"
}
\end{minted}
But for any university login portal that uses other variable names, we can 
simply change the keys in the dictionary:
\begin{minted}{json}
{
  "användarnamn": "dbosk",
  "lösenord": "my secret password"
}
\end{minted}

We can modify the test above to skip the KTH-specific handlers that we 
added, otherwise it remains the same.
Even in this reduced configuration, we still add [[AzureMFA]] so
that Microsoft's number-matching step can be completed during a
successful live login.
<<test functions>>=
